WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.391 --> 00:00:02.351
- Hi, everyone, Sal
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.351 --> 00:00:07.181
Welcome to the Ed Talks live
stream subset of the Homeroom.

00:00:07.181 --> 00:00:11.221
We have a very exciting
conversation with Beth Gonzalez,

00:00:11.221 --> 00:00:13.841
Assistant Superintendent
of Detroit Public Schools.

00:00:13.841 --> 00:00:16.121
So start thinking of questions for Beth,

00:00:16.121 --> 00:00:18.001
and you can put those
in the message boards.

00:00:18.001 --> 00:00:19.361
But before we go into that,

00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:20.721
I'll give my standard announcements.

00:00:20.721 --> 00:00:22.681
Reminder that we are a not-for-profit,

00:00:22.681 --> 00:00:25.011
we can only exist through
philanthropic donations.

00:00:25.011 --> 00:00:26.211
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:26.211 --> 00:00:30.331
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:30.331 --> 00:00:33.151
Any donation makes a huge difference.

00:00:33.151 --> 00:00:37.121
There's good analysis that
even every dollar donated

00:00:37.121 --> 00:00:41.121
to Khan Academy has $400
to $500 of social impact.

00:00:41.121 --> 00:00:44.691
So it's hard to find anything out there

00:00:44.691 --> 00:00:47.091
that gives you that type of social return.

00:00:47.091 --> 00:00:50.391
So hopefully think about
making those donations.

00:00:50.391 --> 00:00:53.471
Special shout out to the organizations

00:00:53.471 --> 00:00:56.101
that stepped up during COVID

00:00:56.101 --> 00:00:59.161
when they saw that need for
Khan Academy was even larger,

00:00:59.161 --> 00:01:02.301
traffic had gone up
dramatically to Khan Academy,

00:01:02.301 --> 00:01:03.881
but we were running higher costs.

00:01:03.881 --> 00:01:06.171
So special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:06.171 --> 00:01:09.441
AT&amp;T, Fastly, and
Novartis, and many others

00:01:09.441 --> 00:01:11.151
for helping us fill that gap.

00:01:11.151 --> 00:01:12.411
But we still need more help,

00:01:12.411 --> 00:01:15.631
especially we still have a
multi-million dollar gap,

00:01:15.631 --> 00:01:17.881
going into 2021.

00:01:17.881 --> 00:01:18.921
Last but not least,

00:01:18.921 --> 00:01:22.491
there's a version of this that
you can get in podcast form,

00:01:22.491 --> 00:01:24.371
Homeroom with Sal the Podcast,

00:01:24.371 --> 00:01:27.131
check that out wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:01:27.131 --> 00:01:30.621
So with that, I'm excited
to introduce Beth Gonzalez,

00:01:30.621 --> 00:01:34.401
Assistant Superintendent
of Detroit Public Schools.

00:01:34.401 --> 00:01:36.241
Beth, welcome.

00:01:36.241 --> 00:01:38.011
- Hi, Sal, thank you so much.

00:01:38.011 --> 00:01:40.364
So happy to be here this afternoon.

00:01:41.371 --> 00:01:43.084
- No, we're so happy to have you.

00:01:45.436 --> 00:01:48.091
You and other administrators like yourself

00:01:48.091 --> 00:01:50.891
or distributors around the
country or in the world really,

00:01:50.891 --> 00:01:55.891
have had a non-enviable job
over the last nine months or so.

00:01:56.201 --> 00:01:58.581
I'd like to hear your narrative

00:01:58.581 --> 00:02:00.691
of how Detroit Public Schools

00:02:00.691 --> 00:02:03.871
has thought about
keeping students learning

00:02:03.871 --> 00:02:05.451
as early as last March

00:02:05.451 --> 00:02:09.101
when the schools had to
start being close physically

00:02:09.101 --> 00:02:11.351
through the summer and
all the way until now.

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:17.784
- Great, yes, our roles
certainly have been dynamic,

00:02:18.741 --> 00:02:21.631
and I have been really fortunate

00:02:21.631 --> 00:02:24.211
to get to connect with other leaders

00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:27.094
and like roles like mine and other cities.

00:02:29.031 --> 00:02:32.714
It's probably one of the
most least anticipated,

00:02:33.591 --> 00:02:34.841
greatest challenges,

00:02:34.841 --> 00:02:39.221
but also a landscape
of opportunity as well.

00:02:39.221 --> 00:02:41.361
So as we navigate through this,

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:43.211
we think really critically

00:02:43.211 --> 00:02:45.751
about what our decisions
mean for students,

00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:48.291
and then what does it mean for right now

00:02:48.291 --> 00:02:53.291
and how can we use this
opportunity long-term as well.

00:02:53.781 --> 00:02:58.781
So in Detroit, it's been a
really interesting journey.

00:02:58.881 --> 00:03:00.961
So beginning in the spring,

00:03:00.961 --> 00:03:03.524
obviously like most districts nationally,

00:03:06.364 --> 00:03:10.344
without any warning sort of, we closed.

00:03:11.226 --> 00:03:16.226
And essentially, initially,
we distributed paper packets

00:03:16.861 --> 00:03:19.091
to students for a three-week period

00:03:19.091 --> 00:03:21.181
that led us up into spring break.

00:03:21.181 --> 00:03:25.171
And then during that,
almost four week timeline,

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:28.611
my team and other teams working
actually in our district,

00:03:28.611 --> 00:03:32.581
about 126 educators and a
number of partner organizations

00:03:32.581 --> 00:03:35.781
really launched what we called
a distance learning plan.

00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:40.658
And that was where we shifted
to online instruction.

00:03:44.181 --> 00:03:45.781
With prerecorded lessons,

00:03:45.781 --> 00:03:47.481
we developed over a thousand of them,

00:03:47.481 --> 00:03:49.701
we leveraged Khan Academy content

00:03:50.858 --> 00:03:54.271
and daily instruction for
a limited period of time

00:03:54.271 --> 00:03:55.411
with our educators,

00:03:55.411 --> 00:03:59.521
building up on a platform
we had already been using,

00:03:59.521 --> 00:04:02.321
but not at scale Microsoft Teams.

00:04:02.321 --> 00:04:06.491
And what we learned in
that time was we were able

00:04:06.491 --> 00:04:10.111
to ensure continuity of learning.

00:04:10.111 --> 00:04:13.811
We built on the high quality
instructional materials

00:04:13.811 --> 00:04:16.361
that we had adopted
over the last few years

00:04:16.361 --> 00:04:19.251
to be able to continue instruction aligned

00:04:19.251 --> 00:04:21.741
to our vision of excellent instruction,

00:04:21.741 --> 00:04:25.881
but all of our students did
not have equitable access

00:04:25.881 --> 00:04:27.571
to that instruction.

00:04:27.571 --> 00:04:29.941
So some students were
watching these YouTube videos

00:04:29.941 --> 00:04:32.831
on phones, participating in class

00:04:32.831 --> 00:04:35.621
through Teams on cellphones, through apps,

00:04:35.621 --> 00:04:38.881
versus actually having a stable computer.

00:04:38.881 --> 00:04:43.491
And that is where in the spring,

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:46.051
our superintendent and
chief strategy officer

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:48.021
and senior level leaders in the district

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:51.111
really started thinking about how do we go

00:04:51.111 --> 00:04:55.441
after this complete, huge
digital divide in the city

00:04:55.441 --> 00:04:59.951
and just frankly, inequity that
still existed in our system

00:04:59.951 --> 00:05:02.111
that wasn't present in other systems

00:05:02.111 --> 00:05:05.931
where students were able to
just go home and use computers.

00:05:05.931 --> 00:05:09.691
And so when we started
leading here in the district,

00:05:09.691 --> 00:05:14.691
the device ratio was at
its best, like six to one,

00:05:16.051 --> 00:05:19.221
making it really difficult
to actually do any at scale,

00:05:19.221 --> 00:05:21.721
digital learning in
classrooms district wide.

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:24.181
So we had been doing
some really intense work

00:05:24.181 --> 00:05:26.911
to move our schools to one-to-one.

00:05:26.911 --> 00:05:29.551
And last spring, we were
in the last deployments

00:05:29.551 --> 00:05:31.541
of our high school.

00:05:31.541 --> 00:05:34.671
But really what our students needed

00:05:34.671 --> 00:05:37.001
was devices in their homes.

00:05:37.001 --> 00:05:40.001
And so we were very thankful

00:05:40.001 --> 00:05:43.951
in working with the
philanthropic community

00:05:43.951 --> 00:05:46.921
to receive a $23 million
investment for an initiative

00:05:46.921 --> 00:05:48.831
that we called Connected Futures.

00:05:48.831 --> 00:05:50.481
And through that, and through summer,

00:05:50.481 --> 00:05:55.481
we deployed over 44,000 devices

00:05:55.791 --> 00:05:57.601
to the students in our district,

00:05:57.601 --> 00:06:01.261
which we sit just about 50,000 students.

00:06:01.261 --> 00:06:06.011
And so through the summer,
as we reached fall,

00:06:06.011 --> 00:06:09.641
we really worked on
closing the digital divide

00:06:09.641 --> 00:06:11.741
in terms of access devices.

00:06:11.741 --> 00:06:15.891
However, that did not mean
that all families and students

00:06:15.891 --> 00:06:19.071
were well-equipped to use that device

00:06:19.071 --> 00:06:20.711
to learn on a daily basis.

00:06:20.711 --> 00:06:25.151
And so while we celebrated
that opportunity,

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:28.121
we've faced a number of challenges,

00:06:28.121 --> 00:06:29.601
even after students had access.

00:06:29.601 --> 00:06:33.601
And the district actually made
about a $4 million investment

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:37.061
in students having access
to internet connectivity

00:06:37.061 --> 00:06:38.411
in their homes.

00:06:38.411 --> 00:06:40.734
And that's a commitment
that we will continue.

00:06:43.041 --> 00:06:44.591
We're constantly reflecting

00:06:44.591 --> 00:06:47.201
on what we've been able to
accomplish, where we are,

00:06:47.201 --> 00:06:49.191
what are the challenges
our students and families

00:06:49.191 --> 00:06:51.201
are still facing.

00:06:51.201 --> 00:06:53.511
Because despite having done that work,

00:06:53.511 --> 00:06:55.241
and there's a lot of cities nationally

00:06:55.241 --> 00:06:57.104
who have like stories,

00:06:58.221 --> 00:07:02.181
and I think that in itself is progress.

00:07:02.181 --> 00:07:03.701
But we still have students

00:07:03.701 --> 00:07:07.061
that aren't able to participate
in school on a daily basis.

00:07:07.061 --> 00:07:11.041
And we've launched a number
of things to address that,

00:07:11.041 --> 00:07:14.314
but certainly have
still more room to grow.

00:07:15.671 --> 00:07:19.201
And so I would describe
that overarchingly,

00:07:19.201 --> 00:07:22.411
as sort of like what's
happened in Detroit.

00:07:22.411 --> 00:07:25.001
And for me, one of the things,

00:07:25.001 --> 00:07:26.641
in my role as assistant superintendent

00:07:26.641 --> 00:07:28.331
for curriculum instruction,

00:07:28.331 --> 00:07:29.911
teaching and learning is at the core,

00:07:29.911 --> 00:07:31.401
the heart of what I do.

00:07:31.401 --> 00:07:35.921
And so we have been really, really focused

00:07:35.921 --> 00:07:39.498
on making sure that our
students have continued access

00:07:39.498 --> 00:07:41.501
and our teachers have continued access

00:07:41.501 --> 00:07:45.421
to high quality instructional materials

00:07:45.421 --> 00:07:50.421
that are highly rated,
that are part of our work,

00:07:50.931 --> 00:07:53.941
and that's part of work
we're really still pursuing

00:07:53.941 --> 00:07:56.281
is culturally relevant materials.

00:07:56.281 --> 00:08:00.641
And so that's a point of pride,
but there are still nuances.

00:08:00.641 --> 00:08:02.031
There's multiple platforms

00:08:02.031 --> 00:08:03.841
that's hard for kids
and hard for families.

00:08:03.841 --> 00:08:07.421
So even as I share celebrations,

00:08:07.421 --> 00:08:12.141
each of those accomplishments are coupled

00:08:12.141 --> 00:08:14.421
with things that we're still working on

00:08:14.421 --> 00:08:17.704
to improve what we're
doing for our students.

00:08:19.133 --> 00:08:20.940
- And there's a lot in that.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.621
And once again, thank
you for the heroic effort

00:08:22.621 --> 00:08:24.181
that you and your fellow district leaders

00:08:24.181 --> 00:08:25.741
have been doing it.

00:08:25.741 --> 00:08:28.801
I'm curious about that percentage of kids

00:08:28.801 --> 00:08:30.291
or whatever the percentage is

00:08:30.291 --> 00:08:33.251
that haven't been able to engage.

00:08:33.251 --> 00:08:34.621
How large has that been?

00:08:34.621 --> 00:08:36.821
We've been hearing that
from a lot of districts.

00:08:36.821 --> 00:08:40.231
I live in a middle income,
upper-middle income suburban,

00:08:40.231 --> 00:08:41.681
small school districts,

00:08:41.681 --> 00:08:43.891
a school teacher shares
the cul-de-sac with me,

00:08:43.891 --> 00:08:45.971
she was telling me that
5%, 10% of her kids

00:08:45.971 --> 00:08:47.061
haven't been engaged.

00:08:47.061 --> 00:08:49.421
I could imagine the problem
could be worse than Detroit.

00:08:49.421 --> 00:08:50.911
How big is the problem there

00:08:50.911 --> 00:08:52.911
and what are the best explanations

00:08:52.911 --> 00:08:54.581
for why these kids haven't
been able to engage,

00:08:54.581 --> 00:08:57.384
and what are y'all trying
to keep them engaged?

00:08:58.811 --> 00:09:00.731
- Yeah, a great question.

00:09:00.731 --> 00:09:03.271
We've really been looking
at student engagement

00:09:03.271 --> 00:09:07.074
and attendance from a number
of different perspectives.

00:09:09.501 --> 00:09:11.191
Our enrollment actually, as a district,

00:09:11.191 --> 00:09:13.134
has continued to increase.

00:09:14.101 --> 00:09:17.571
And we were making really
significant progress

00:09:17.571 --> 00:09:21.981
on both reducing the number of
chronically-absent students,

00:09:21.981 --> 00:09:23.331
increasing enrollment

00:09:23.331 --> 00:09:26.931
and increasing just healthy
daily attendance rates

00:09:26.931 --> 00:09:29.541
district-wide with some exemplar schools.

00:09:29.541 --> 00:09:32.671
But the pandemic has really impacted,

00:09:32.671 --> 00:09:35.741
and our data looks similar to what it did

00:09:35.741 --> 00:09:37.514
about two years ago.

00:09:42.646 --> 00:09:44.313
It's not consistent.

00:09:46.615 --> 00:09:48.491
We do have some students
who are not coming at all,

00:09:48.491 --> 00:09:52.031
but it's inconsistent in
terms of period of the day

00:09:52.031 --> 00:09:53.434
or day of the week.

00:09:55.857 --> 00:09:57.591
So we're thinking about that in two ways.

00:09:57.591 --> 00:10:01.791
The first is really intentional outreach.

00:10:01.791 --> 00:10:05.461
And so we've leveraged all school staff

00:10:06.351 --> 00:10:08.611
to reach out to families,
to do well checks,

00:10:08.611 --> 00:10:13.291
to offer supports beyond
just academic support.

00:10:13.291 --> 00:10:15.381
So we're thinking about the
mental health of the family

00:10:15.381 --> 00:10:18.371
or what additional needs
they may have that,

00:10:18.371 --> 00:10:22.051
whether is food insecurity
or utility assistance.

00:10:22.051 --> 00:10:23.791
And our school culture facilitators

00:10:23.791 --> 00:10:26.581
and our deans and our school staff,

00:10:26.581 --> 00:10:29.881
it's all hands on deck trying
to reach out to families

00:10:29.881 --> 00:10:33.391
to understand why their students
may not be participating.

00:10:33.391 --> 00:10:37.521
Beyond that, we have a team
of district team members

00:10:37.521 --> 00:10:39.421
who led a large effort,

00:10:39.421 --> 00:10:43.441
including district volunteers
and others to canvas,

00:10:43.441 --> 00:10:45.441
and to really go to every home

00:10:46.451 --> 00:10:49.944
for our students who haven't showed up.

00:10:50.781 --> 00:10:53.791
And that was work I actually did myself

00:10:53.791 --> 00:10:54.931
in our neighborhoods,

00:10:54.931 --> 00:10:57.941
meeting families and talking
to them face to face,

00:10:57.941 --> 00:11:01.531
of course, outside, socially
distance, safely with masks on,

00:11:01.531 --> 00:11:04.281
about what challenges they're facing

00:11:05.355 --> 00:11:06.451
and communicating with them

00:11:06.451 --> 00:11:10.371
to make sure they understand
their child does have a device,

00:11:10.371 --> 00:11:11.911
their school's waiting for them,

00:11:11.911 --> 00:11:15.351
and we're eager to provide
instruction and supports

00:11:15.351 --> 00:11:16.564
to their family.

00:11:17.401 --> 00:11:18.721
We now are at a place

00:11:18.721 --> 00:11:21.891
where it is really about
home visits and outreach.

00:11:21.891 --> 00:11:26.081
And so despite the increase
infection rates in the city

00:11:26.081 --> 00:11:28.874
and the challenges in terms
of being face-to-face,

00:11:30.811 --> 00:11:34.751
we're trying to engage
students as much as possible,

00:11:34.751 --> 00:11:37.911
and we're also studying the
impact on their performance.

00:11:37.911 --> 00:11:40.931
So this is a national
conversation that's emerged.

00:11:40.931 --> 00:11:43.711
I know a number of other school districts

00:11:43.711 --> 00:11:46.951
are grappling with this,
but we have studied,

00:11:46.951 --> 00:11:49.371
and we know that
district-wide, when we look

00:11:49.371 --> 00:11:53.821
at quarter one performance
of our students in 2019

00:11:53.821 --> 00:11:56.961
versus quarter one performance
of our students in 2020,

00:11:56.961 --> 00:12:01.191
we have had a 15% increase
in F grades district-wide.

00:12:01.191 --> 00:12:04.241
There is a variance across schools

00:12:04.241 --> 00:12:07.104
with actually decreases,

00:12:08.788 --> 00:12:11.311
particularly in some of our lower grades,

00:12:11.311 --> 00:12:14.721
and also increases in
some of our high schools

00:12:14.721 --> 00:12:17.811
where we're seeing larger
total numbers of students

00:12:17.811 --> 00:12:18.644
with Fs.

00:12:18.644 --> 00:12:20.451
And so that's really pushing us

00:12:20.451 --> 00:12:22.881
to think about student engagement

00:12:22.881 --> 00:12:24.271
in a couple of different ways.

00:12:24.271 --> 00:12:27.561
Like how do we support kids
to make sure they're coming,

00:12:27.561 --> 00:12:28.981
and then when they're coming,

00:12:28.981 --> 00:12:32.511
how do we support them to be successful

00:12:32.511 --> 00:12:34.711
in this completely new
learning environment?

00:12:35.711 --> 00:12:37.761
- When you did some of these home visits,

00:12:38.831 --> 00:12:40.891
what did people tell you why
their kids weren't showing up

00:12:40.891 --> 00:12:45.221
to the Microsoft Teams classroom?

00:12:45.221 --> 00:12:48.041
What were the explanations
you've been hearing?

00:12:48.041 --> 00:12:51.261
- Yeah, there are a number of reasons

00:12:51.261 --> 00:12:52.861
which are very legitimate.

00:12:52.861 --> 00:12:56.531
So some families faced challenges

00:12:56.531 --> 00:12:59.221
with the device they originally received

00:12:59.221 --> 00:13:01.481
having already been broken

00:13:01.481 --> 00:13:05.561
and not feeling like they
had a resources or a solution

00:13:05.561 --> 00:13:08.061
in order to have that fixed.

00:13:08.061 --> 00:13:10.171
And in order to address that, actually,

00:13:10.171 --> 00:13:13.921
the district recently
launched 13 regional hubs

00:13:13.921 --> 00:13:15.241
throughout the city

00:13:15.241 --> 00:13:18.891
where we're providing a
number of in-person supports

00:13:18.891 --> 00:13:22.251
to families, including
actually, device repair,

00:13:22.251 --> 00:13:26.421
online learning support,
mental health resources.

00:13:26.421 --> 00:13:31.421
Actually, we are offering COVID
testing to support families.

00:13:32.311 --> 00:13:37.261
And so to help make that
not continue to be an issue.

00:13:37.261 --> 00:13:39.931
In the early stages of this work,

00:13:39.931 --> 00:13:43.741
when I was out in
neighborhoods, in some ways,

00:13:43.741 --> 00:13:45.981
families just didn't feel

00:13:45.981 --> 00:13:50.981
like they had all of the clear
communication and information

00:13:52.411 --> 00:13:54.351
about what was available to them,

00:13:54.351 --> 00:13:57.491
what they were supposed to
do, how they to participate.

00:13:57.491 --> 00:14:00.051
And so that's why the home
visits were so important

00:14:00.051 --> 00:14:02.501
because it really helped us, face-to-face,

00:14:02.501 --> 00:14:04.131
describe to someone what to do,

00:14:04.131 --> 00:14:05.871
get their cellphone number

00:14:05.871 --> 00:14:09.074
and just solve the
problem alongside of them.

00:14:10.641 --> 00:14:13.611
We also launched an online
learning help hotline.

00:14:13.611 --> 00:14:18.611
We've had over 2,000 calls to date there

00:14:18.611 --> 00:14:22.738
so that families can to call in and say,

00:14:22.738 --> 00:14:24.621
"Gosh, I need help this isn't working.

00:14:24.621 --> 00:14:25.951
I don't know what to do.

00:14:25.951 --> 00:14:30.951
How do I enroll now that
so much is so different?"

00:14:31.471 --> 00:14:32.781
So those are a few of the things

00:14:32.781 --> 00:14:33.901
that families shared with us

00:14:33.901 --> 00:14:35.901
and some of the things that we've done

00:14:35.901 --> 00:14:38.304
to try to support them and address that.

00:14:39.501 --> 00:14:41.871
- So it sounds like
it's, in some categories,

00:14:41.871 --> 00:14:43.591
just tech difficulties

00:14:43.591 --> 00:14:46.501
and then probably a little
bit of an activation energy

00:14:46.501 --> 00:14:51.501
of just not knowing how to
engage in this new world.

00:14:52.181 --> 00:14:53.014
On the other issue,

00:14:53.014 --> 00:14:55.831
you mentioned the students
failing disproportionately,

00:14:55.831 --> 00:14:58.081
especially at the high school level.

00:14:58.081 --> 00:14:59.361
How do y'all categorize that?

00:14:59.361 --> 00:15:00.931
How much of that is the access issue?

00:15:00.931 --> 00:15:02.291
We just talked about how much of that

00:15:02.291 --> 00:15:04.881
is kind of stress and mental health

00:15:04.881 --> 00:15:06.931
and anxiety and depression

00:15:06.931 --> 00:15:09.364
and how much of that
is just something else.

00:15:11.401 --> 00:15:15.121
- So we're actually studying
the relationship right now

00:15:15.121 --> 00:15:17.191
between student performance and attendance

00:15:17.191 --> 00:15:22.191
to see what the relationship is there.

00:15:23.091 --> 00:15:26.491
We've also been actively
engaging in focus groups

00:15:26.491 --> 00:15:29.421
with our students to hear from them,

00:15:29.421 --> 00:15:33.171
what their perceptions
are about this challenge,

00:15:33.171 --> 00:15:36.461
what ideas they have, how they feel.

00:15:36.461 --> 00:15:41.461
We've heard a number of things
from students, themselves.

00:15:43.941 --> 00:15:48.451
Students have articulated
that this is hard on them,

00:15:48.451 --> 00:15:50.861
and it's hard on their teachers

00:15:50.861 --> 00:15:55.861
that they sometimes are challenged

00:15:56.181 --> 00:15:59.701
by the number of platforms
and what's required of them

00:15:59.701 --> 00:16:00.991
to submit assignments.

00:16:00.991 --> 00:16:03.591
They talked about examples
where they had submitted work,

00:16:03.591 --> 00:16:06.261
but their teacher didn't receive it.

00:16:06.261 --> 00:16:09.321
They talked about the expectation
for submission of work,

00:16:09.321 --> 00:16:12.521
feeling as though we're not in school,

00:16:12.521 --> 00:16:15.521
we're at home, we're online,

00:16:15.521 --> 00:16:19.151
but my teacher's expectation
for turning things in on time

00:16:19.151 --> 00:16:20.281
hasn't really changed,

00:16:20.281 --> 00:16:23.551
and it's really not
sensitive to where I am,

00:16:23.551 --> 00:16:27.131
in terms of having to provide
care for a younger sibling

00:16:27.131 --> 00:16:32.131
or having to be a caregiver in my own home

00:16:32.181 --> 00:16:35.451
or having other responsibilities.

00:16:35.451 --> 00:16:37.771
So I think students are
facing a number of things.

00:16:37.771 --> 00:16:40.271
We also heard about from
kids, just like you said, Sal,

00:16:40.271 --> 00:16:44.261
where really, their mental
health is suffering.

00:16:44.261 --> 00:16:47.224
They feel isolated, they feel alone.

00:16:48.991 --> 00:16:51.691
They say, "I wake up in
the morning, it's dark.

00:16:51.691 --> 00:16:55.414
I get on my computer, I
finish school, it's dark,

00:16:56.683 --> 00:17:00.961
and I don't have the
same social engagement."

00:17:00.961 --> 00:17:05.961
So one thing I didn't speak
to earlier was our district

00:17:06.351 --> 00:17:08.741
was actually one of the
first school districts.

00:17:08.741 --> 00:17:11.431
Now, I think it was the first
school district nationally,

00:17:11.431 --> 00:17:14.261
certainly large urban
to welcome students back

00:17:14.261 --> 00:17:16.781
for in-person instruction
after the spring closure.

00:17:16.781 --> 00:17:18.631
So we started preparing

00:17:18.631 --> 00:17:20.921
and launched in-person
instruction in summer

00:17:22.211 --> 00:17:26.621
and had only like three cases
in our schools at that time.

00:17:26.621 --> 00:17:28.501
And then opened the school year

00:17:28.501 --> 00:17:30.651
with three different
sort of implementations.

00:17:30.651 --> 00:17:33.501
One was an in-person
opportunity for learning,

00:17:33.501 --> 00:17:35.871
matching teachers who were
willing to teach in-person

00:17:35.871 --> 00:17:37.981
with students who wanted
to learn in-person

00:17:37.981 --> 00:17:40.661
or whose families wanted
them to learn in-person.

00:17:40.661 --> 00:17:42.381
We also launched a learning centers.

00:17:42.381 --> 00:17:44.071
So these students were learning online,

00:17:44.071 --> 00:17:46.491
but were able to come to
an educational environment

00:17:46.491 --> 00:17:48.241
with the support of other educators,

00:17:48.241 --> 00:17:50.661
like dean school, culture facilitators,

00:17:50.661 --> 00:17:52.981
assistant principals, paraeducators,

00:17:52.981 --> 00:17:54.391
academic interventionists,

00:17:54.391 --> 00:17:56.171
to make sure that they have
the support they needed

00:17:56.171 --> 00:17:59.511
for the assignment submission
and the online participation.

00:17:59.511 --> 00:18:03.881
And then we also had
almost 80% of our students

00:18:03.881 --> 00:18:06.051
participating just online.

00:18:06.051 --> 00:18:10.421
But unfortunately, because of
increases in infection rates

00:18:10.421 --> 00:18:11.601
at the very end of November,

00:18:11.601 --> 00:18:15.341
we had to shift to just
online learning again.

00:18:15.341 --> 00:18:17.691
And so these challenges

00:18:17.691 --> 00:18:20.281
that some of our high
school students are facing

00:18:20.281 --> 00:18:23.321
are actually increasing, not decreasing,

00:18:23.321 --> 00:18:26.791
with their siblings not being
able to go to learning centers

00:18:28.201 --> 00:18:30.551
and learn, and them if they benefited

00:18:30.551 --> 00:18:31.891
from it learning in-person.

00:18:31.891 --> 00:18:35.791
So that's why we're talking
to them to learn from them

00:18:35.791 --> 00:18:37.661
about the challenges they're facing

00:18:37.661 --> 00:18:41.241
and to move to strategies that make sense

00:18:41.241 --> 00:18:43.381
for a pathway to success,

00:18:43.381 --> 00:18:46.111
including supporting our
own educators and guidance

00:18:46.111 --> 00:18:49.374
on equitable grading
practices, for example.

00:18:50.671 --> 00:18:51.761
- I mean, on that last piece,

00:18:51.761 --> 00:18:53.821
this is something we've always
talked about at Khan Academy,

00:18:53.821 --> 00:18:55.951
pre pandemic, this social mastery learning

00:18:55.951 --> 00:18:58.771
that if you get a C or B or an F,

00:18:58.771 --> 00:19:02.331
that you should always have
an opportunity to remediate

00:19:02.331 --> 00:19:05.091
and get it to an A level,

00:19:05.091 --> 00:19:07.361
especially when COVID seems important,

00:19:07.361 --> 00:19:09.951
because we all know what it
does to a student's self-esteem.

00:19:09.951 --> 00:19:12.211
And frankly, if they
have a gap in something,

00:19:12.211 --> 00:19:13.481
they should be able to fill it in,

00:19:13.481 --> 00:19:15.751
otherwise, they'll have trouble
with more advanced topics.

00:19:15.751 --> 00:19:20.011
Do you see mechanisms by which y'all can,

00:19:20.011 --> 00:19:22.054
these kids who are failing
or getting worse grades

00:19:22.054 --> 00:19:25.391
that they normally would, to
be able to do makeup work,

00:19:25.391 --> 00:19:27.231
so to speak, to maybe improve their grades

00:19:27.231 --> 00:19:30.031
so it doesn't have a lasting
implication for their life.

00:19:31.391 --> 00:19:33.451
- Yeah, in fact, what you're describing

00:19:33.451 --> 00:19:36.051
is something I'm really
personally passionate about.

00:19:36.051 --> 00:19:39.821
I was a math educator
and so much of our work,

00:19:39.821 --> 00:19:41.904
leading up into this pandemic,

00:19:43.319 --> 00:19:45.781
with the current student
performance in the district,

00:19:45.781 --> 00:19:48.751
we have a number of students already

00:19:48.751 --> 00:19:51.851
who would be considered
one or more years behind.

00:19:51.851 --> 00:19:56.421
And so a really huge part of our work

00:19:56.421 --> 00:19:58.581
is providing tiered interventions

00:19:58.581 --> 00:20:00.291
to those students and making sure

00:20:00.291 --> 00:20:03.781
that they have the academic time and space

00:20:03.781 --> 00:20:06.121
to do that work, beyond just continuing

00:20:06.121 --> 00:20:08.911
with high quality grade level instruction.

00:20:08.911 --> 00:20:12.091
And so we do have a foundation

00:20:12.091 --> 00:20:14.241
around what you specifically spoke to,

00:20:14.241 --> 00:20:15.854
which is course recovery.

00:20:17.131 --> 00:20:19.441
We've done work over
the last couple of years

00:20:19.441 --> 00:20:22.704
to have strong course
recovery structures in place.

00:20:24.041 --> 00:20:25.211
Over the last summer,

00:20:25.211 --> 00:20:28.274
we had students recover about 600 courses.

00:20:29.393 --> 00:20:33.051
And that's something that we'll build upon

00:20:34.151 --> 00:20:35.551
as we move forward.

00:20:35.551 --> 00:20:37.591
I think our job right now,

00:20:37.591 --> 00:20:38.891
given where we are in the school year,

00:20:38.891 --> 00:20:41.591
is to think about an enact strategies

00:20:41.591 --> 00:20:44.211
to prevent some of that with our educators

00:20:44.211 --> 00:20:45.431
so that that work can be done

00:20:45.431 --> 00:20:50.391
before students find
themselves in that space.

00:20:50.391 --> 00:20:53.521
And so not only are we doing work

00:20:53.521 --> 00:20:55.681
for success with grade level instruction,

00:20:55.681 --> 00:20:58.891
but we're also actually
expanding and pursuing

00:20:58.891 --> 00:21:03.621
with this unique structure,
with differentiated schedules,

00:21:03.621 --> 00:21:04.961
with things being online.

00:21:04.961 --> 00:21:07.981
There are actually also opportunities

00:21:07.981 --> 00:21:11.781
to provide direct services
and interventions to students

00:21:11.781 --> 00:21:13.291
in a way that...

00:21:14.631 --> 00:21:15.621
We can do in-person,

00:21:15.621 --> 00:21:19.641
but the online structures have some value.

00:21:19.641 --> 00:21:20.661
In fact, in our high school,

00:21:20.661 --> 00:21:24.321
we have students really
need reading interventions.

00:21:24.321 --> 00:21:26.741
I mean, I realized at that point,

00:21:26.741 --> 00:21:29.781
most students are using reading as a tool

00:21:29.781 --> 00:21:32.291
to learn and achieve
in their core courses,

00:21:32.291 --> 00:21:33.771
but we've made a deep commitment

00:21:33.771 --> 00:21:35.381
to addressing students' learning gaps.

00:21:35.381 --> 00:21:39.594
So we're launching and we've done work.

00:21:40.731 --> 00:21:41.951
District-wide, in fact,

00:21:41.951 --> 00:21:45.591
our goal is to reach over
9,000 students in tier three,

00:21:45.591 --> 00:21:48.771
reading interventions,
using this opportunity

00:21:48.771 --> 00:21:50.981
and being able to flexibly group them.

00:21:50.981 --> 00:21:53.191
'Cause they're not in a
brick and mortar building

00:21:53.191 --> 00:21:55.361
in one space, they can only
come together at one time,

00:21:55.361 --> 00:21:59.571
we can actually pull across
schools and across classrooms

00:21:59.571 --> 00:22:01.501
to do some deep interventions,

00:22:01.501 --> 00:22:03.651
whether those be
one-on-one or three-on-one.

00:22:03.651 --> 00:22:07.681
So this is all work that we
have underway, we're pursuing,

00:22:07.681 --> 00:22:09.841
and we just hope to expand,

00:22:09.841 --> 00:22:14.841
and to your point, to continue
to leverage the resources,

00:22:15.011 --> 00:22:17.524
like Khan Academy and
math to help support that.

00:22:18.981 --> 00:22:21.091
- Yeah, no, the dream
scenario is the students

00:22:21.091 --> 00:22:24.191
show mastery on Khan Academy
that y'all give them.

00:22:24.191 --> 00:22:27.181
Give them credit, but we
can talk about that offline.

00:22:27.181 --> 00:22:30.801
There's a question related
to this from a YouTube niche,

00:22:30.801 --> 00:22:32.601
seven, eight, six,

00:22:32.601 --> 00:22:34.411
and there's actually
three questions in here,

00:22:34.411 --> 00:22:35.861
each of which are pretty big.

00:22:37.901 --> 00:22:39.601
They're asking, does
online school work well

00:22:39.601 --> 00:22:41.841
for both teachers and students?

00:22:41.841 --> 00:22:42.674
We've talked a little bit

00:22:42.674 --> 00:22:44.611
about where it's not working
so well for students.

00:22:44.611 --> 00:22:46.921
I am curious about the
teacher side of things.

00:22:46.921 --> 00:22:48.051
What are you hearing from teachers

00:22:48.051 --> 00:22:50.301
about what's not working for them

00:22:50.301 --> 00:22:52.494
or are there some things that
are working well for them?

00:22:53.991 --> 00:22:55.364
- Yeah, great question.

00:22:57.291 --> 00:23:00.791
I think if I answered
that with a broad stroke,

00:23:00.791 --> 00:23:05.081
a yes or a no, I would not be
attending to the nuance here

00:23:05.081 --> 00:23:08.741
because obviously, our teaching force,

00:23:08.741 --> 00:23:10.191
like other teachers especially,

00:23:10.191 --> 00:23:13.491
we're a diverse set of educators

00:23:13.491 --> 00:23:17.441
with lots of different
experiences and perspectives.

00:23:17.441 --> 00:23:22.441
And so the things that
I've heard from educators

00:23:23.151 --> 00:23:25.941
that are working well,

00:23:25.941 --> 00:23:29.521
one is, obviously we are in a pandemic,

00:23:29.521 --> 00:23:33.101
and so there are a number of educators

00:23:33.101 --> 00:23:36.061
who have clearly expressed their concerns

00:23:36.061 --> 00:23:37.511
about teaching in-person.

00:23:37.511 --> 00:23:42.511
And so I think the opportunity
to teach students online,

00:23:45.371 --> 00:23:47.441
educators are thankful
for that opportunity

00:23:47.441 --> 00:23:49.744
for their own safety.

00:23:51.241 --> 00:23:55.271
But I will say we also have
had a strong group of educators

00:23:55.271 --> 00:23:56.161
teach in-person,

00:23:56.161 --> 00:24:00.421
and we've invested in a
significant amount of PPE

00:24:00.421 --> 00:24:04.331
and resources to help make
those safe environments

00:24:04.331 --> 00:24:06.824
for educators and students.

00:24:08.161 --> 00:24:12.771
Teachers have said they
feel supported by us.

00:24:12.771 --> 00:24:16.191
So we adopted a learning
management system called Schoology.

00:24:16.191 --> 00:24:21.191
We ensured that we procured
and provided to educators

00:24:22.311 --> 00:24:25.781
all of the online options
for their existing programs.

00:24:25.781 --> 00:24:27.951
So we didn't force them to
learn something completely new

00:24:27.951 --> 00:24:28.991
just to do it online,

00:24:28.991 --> 00:24:33.251
we really transitioned
their existing content

00:24:33.251 --> 00:24:34.861
to a digital platform.

00:24:34.861 --> 00:24:39.551
We also complimented the
use of Schoology with Teams

00:24:39.551 --> 00:24:42.121
and have made iterative improvements there

00:24:42.121 --> 00:24:44.001
for teachers to deliver instruction.

00:24:44.001 --> 00:24:46.771
And we've really put our
teachers at the center,

00:24:46.771 --> 00:24:50.961
meaning them delivering
instruction each day to students

00:24:50.961 --> 00:24:53.794
we think is a real priority
in our implementation.

00:24:54.751 --> 00:24:57.221
I think teachers have grown themselves

00:24:57.221 --> 00:24:59.841
in their digital
literacies and capacities,

00:24:59.841 --> 00:25:01.551
and they actually see growth in kids.

00:25:01.551 --> 00:25:03.421
And especially our teachers

00:25:03.421 --> 00:25:05.701
who are serving our youngest students,

00:25:05.701 --> 00:25:07.101
it's really fun to talk to them

00:25:07.101 --> 00:25:09.572
about the things kids
have done on computers

00:25:09.572 --> 00:25:13.261
and online platforms
where they've had success

00:25:13.261 --> 00:25:16.251
that they didn't anticipate.

00:25:16.251 --> 00:25:19.651
And teachers still feel deeply
connected to their students.

00:25:19.651 --> 00:25:21.694
So outside of using a computer,

00:25:22.581 --> 00:25:25.881
they are connecting with them.

00:25:25.881 --> 00:25:29.121
I talked to teachers who
go to their students' homes

00:25:29.121 --> 00:25:33.131
to bring them things and
work to make them successful.

00:25:33.131 --> 00:25:37.331
So I think educators still
feel equivalently committed

00:25:37.331 --> 00:25:39.374
to their own students.

00:25:41.691 --> 00:25:42.911
It varies.

00:25:42.911 --> 00:25:44.841
Different kids have different supports,

00:25:44.841 --> 00:25:46.884
different prior experiences.

00:25:48.071 --> 00:25:50.501
And so there are strong
examples of students

00:25:50.501 --> 00:25:54.001
who learned new 21st century skills

00:25:54.001 --> 00:25:55.651
that they didn't previously have.

00:25:56.891 --> 00:25:57.991
We're a Microsoft district,

00:25:57.991 --> 00:26:02.811
so we've leveraged like Class Notebook,

00:26:02.811 --> 00:26:07.161
Microsoft Whiteboard,
and a number of other...

00:26:07.161 --> 00:26:09.171
I'm not coming up with all the platforms,

00:26:09.171 --> 00:26:12.521
but our kids have learned
to use these things.

00:26:12.521 --> 00:26:15.964
And honestly, they're things,
as a professional, I use also.

00:26:16.891 --> 00:26:18.321
And I think some of our students

00:26:18.321 --> 00:26:21.434
have enjoyed a little bit of autonomy.

00:26:22.684 --> 00:26:27.401
And so I will say there
are positive things

00:26:27.401 --> 00:26:29.891
that will have long-term implications

00:26:29.891 --> 00:26:33.391
for what in-person learning looks like.

00:26:33.391 --> 00:26:35.471
Because I think kids will find

00:26:35.471 --> 00:26:39.211
just writing a notebook
paper potentially archaic

00:26:39.211 --> 00:26:41.011
now that they have their own device.

00:26:42.339 --> 00:26:45.031
And there are struggles

00:26:45.031 --> 00:26:47.801
for educators and for teachers in this.

00:26:47.801 --> 00:26:49.611
But we actually, as educators,

00:26:49.611 --> 00:26:50.891
I'm really glad that question was asked

00:26:50.891 --> 00:26:53.511
because we're so focused on like,

00:26:53.511 --> 00:26:54.931
what are we gonna do better,

00:26:54.931 --> 00:26:56.421
but we do need to also reflect

00:26:56.421 --> 00:26:59.261
on what kinds of things
have benefited students.

00:26:59.261 --> 00:27:01.381
And I'll name one last thing as a benefit,

00:27:01.381 --> 00:27:04.731
and that is expanded equitable access.

00:27:04.731 --> 00:27:07.231
So we've actually been able
to expand students' access

00:27:07.231 --> 00:27:08.641
to dual enrollment in the district

00:27:08.641 --> 00:27:10.341
with our university partners.

00:27:10.341 --> 00:27:11.871
Because it wasn't happening in-person,

00:27:11.871 --> 00:27:14.111
so we weren't limited to just one school.

00:27:14.111 --> 00:27:16.741
So we have students
participating across schools,

00:27:16.741 --> 00:27:19.601
and so we've been able
to serve more students.

00:27:19.601 --> 00:27:24.091
And so I think that also has
implications district-wide

00:27:24.091 --> 00:27:25.691
for when we return.

00:27:25.691 --> 00:27:28.091
How do we make sure every
student in the district

00:27:28.091 --> 00:27:33.091
who wants to take calc three can

00:27:33.371 --> 00:27:34.934
with a university professor?

00:27:36.641 --> 00:27:37.474
- No, that's great.

00:27:37.474 --> 00:27:41.311
Well, these conversations always
go by faster than I expect.

00:27:41.311 --> 00:27:43.811
Thank you so much for joining us,

00:27:43.811 --> 00:27:45.381
and frankly, thank you for the work

00:27:45.381 --> 00:27:46.871
that you and all of the other educators,

00:27:46.871 --> 00:27:48.261
not just in Detroit, but around the world

00:27:48.261 --> 00:27:51.751
are doing right now to keep kids learning.

00:27:51.751 --> 00:27:55.501
But it shows that y'all are
doing, I would say, better

00:27:55.501 --> 00:27:57.371
than what we could hope for,

00:27:57.371 --> 00:27:59.561
given how strains and circumstances are.

00:27:59.561 --> 00:28:03.441
And there's a lot to worry
about with these kids who seem

00:28:03.441 --> 00:28:06.101
to be kind of falling off
and failing or not engaged,

00:28:06.101 --> 00:28:08.491
but there's many things to be hopeful for,

00:28:08.491 --> 00:28:09.971
and it's because of your tireless efforts.

00:28:09.971 --> 00:28:11.581
So thank you for that.

00:28:11.581 --> 00:28:13.461
- No, thank you, Sal.

00:28:13.461 --> 00:28:14.601
I get to say these things,

00:28:14.601 --> 00:28:16.674
but I certainly do not do them alone.

00:28:17.819 --> 00:28:21.771
And our superintendent's
leadership through this time

00:28:21.771 --> 00:28:23.961
and actually our school boards
leadership during this time,

00:28:23.961 --> 00:28:27.821
like everyone in our
organization is deeply focused

00:28:27.821 --> 00:28:31.024
on putting students first.

00:28:32.073 --> 00:28:35.271
And we're committed to continue

00:28:35.271 --> 00:28:40.271
to problem solve with the
community and elevate solutions

00:28:40.481 --> 00:28:43.751
to improve what they're experiencing now

00:28:43.751 --> 00:28:46.341
and support them in the years to come.

00:28:46.341 --> 00:28:50.858
So thank you so much for
having me, and I appreciate it.

00:28:54.121 --> 00:28:55.354
- Thanks for joining us.

00:28:56.481 --> 00:28:59.831
Well, thank you, everyone,
for joining this conversation.

00:28:59.831 --> 00:29:02.861
Hope you got as much out of that as I did,

00:29:02.861 --> 00:29:05.301
obviously there's a lot of
things to be worried about

00:29:05.301 --> 00:29:06.491
because of COVID.

00:29:06.491 --> 00:29:07.411
Some of these were trends

00:29:07.411 --> 00:29:08.751
that were going on even before COVID

00:29:08.751 --> 00:29:11.751
and they've just gotten worse
with kids gaps, et cetera.

00:29:11.751 --> 00:29:14.561
But there's some really
good leadership out there

00:29:14.561 --> 00:29:17.791
in the country, in the world
that is making some efforts

00:29:17.791 --> 00:29:18.981
and I think thinking about things

00:29:18.981 --> 00:29:19.991
in really interesting ways.

00:29:19.991 --> 00:29:22.021
So thanks for joining this week.

00:29:22.021 --> 00:29:24.261
This was the last Homeroom for this week.

00:29:24.261 --> 00:29:27.441
And we might take a little bit
of a hiatus for the holidays.

00:29:27.441 --> 00:29:30.001
So depending, but it might be the new year

00:29:30.001 --> 00:29:31.171
before we see each other again.

00:29:31.171 --> 00:29:34.001
So happy holidays.

00:29:34.001 --> 00:29:35.176
See you later.

00:29:35.176 --> 00:29:37.676
(text chimes)

